Many high-performance workstations and servers are "pickier" about memory than
the run-of-the-mill Pentium 166. DEC has a listed of test/approved memory, and
other memory works. I've heard that Samsung and DataRam work well, Lucky-Goldstar
does not. Just make sure you can return it if it doesn't work.

Before buying peripherals, make sure (that is, ask if) they'll work with the OS
you want to use (ie, there's driver support). Many higher-end cards work with NT.
Just ask the seller or manufacturer, or for Linux, lurk in the newsgroups and ask
around first.

Case & power supply, like with any motherboard, depend on the board itself. The
SX board is ATX, some older boards may need a more "unique" power supply and may
not be ATX form factor. Things like hard drives, CD-ROMs, and monitors are pretty
much universal. Nothing special unless you cram a bunch of big hard drives in
there, in which case you'll need more wattage!

No AGP yet, so stick with PCI. There is 64-bit PCI support on some motherboards
(fiber channel SCSI, gigabit ethernet, etc.), but that's not too common yet (can
use 32-bit PCI in these slots, too). Brand-new RX board supports USB (I think)
and UDMA (I think), but previous versions don't (Ultra DMA drives will work, but
at 16 and not 33). Plain-old ISA support is there, too.

All told (tolled?), it's not that hard. Just a little more "homework" than with
x86 and Windows 95. ;^)

