It is actually possible, though it may not seem so.  I decided in October
that I was going to build an Alpha, unfortunately I started from scratch.
My primary goal was to get a good system for as absolutely cheap as was
possible.  Documentation on this sort of stuff was very hard to find (or
at least very cleverly eluded me).  I wasn't sure what state Linux support
for the Alpha was in, and I knew absolutely nothing about the hardware.
It took me nearly 2 months to get my system up and running.  I made scores
of phone calls, and spent coutless hours browsing the internet for helpful
information.

Here's what I found out:

PURCHASING:
1. If you want cheap, you've got to invest a deal of time on the web.
2. There are some companies who offer prebuilt alpha systems at pretty
good prices, you might consider them if you don't mind spending extra.
3. Most vendors don't want to sell you just a motherboard and processor,
but I found a couple that will.
4. Mail order from out of state, you can save a lot in sales tax.

HARDWARE:
5. If you're going to use linux, don't worry about hardware.  The Alphas
were designed to use PC hardware, so a lot of stuff works.  But don't buy
just anything, make sure you buy popular components that other Alpha linux
users report success with.
6. You can get your componets for fairly cheap, but again check with other
Alpha linux users to find out which models work.  The Digital and Redhat
hardware compatibility lists are inconsitent and usually only list the
expensive models.
7. You'll definitely want SCSI, and there are a lot of supported
controllers for fairly cheap.
8. Get good memory. You'll need to get a very specific type of memory, one
that is a little more costly than the cheap stuff.  If you look around you
can find a decent price (and a lifetime warranty).

SOFTWARE:
9. AlphaBIOS makes me mad.
10. The firmware setup is actually pretty cool once you figure it out.
There are 2 firmware console options for the newer Alphas: AlphaBIOS and
SRM.  AlphaBIOS is intended for those who wish to stoop to the level of
Windows NT.  SRM is intended for Digital UNIX and is really cool.
Unfortunately AlphaBIOS is about as useful as Windows NT, and SRM isn't
free.  There are also at least 4 ways to load linux depending on what
firmware you are using (some methods are better than others).  You won't
[easily] find sufficient documentation on any of this.
11. Linux support is now very good for the Alpha.  The 2.1.X kernels
compile without any patches, 2.2.X should iron out any small bugs that may
exist.
12. Software support for Alpha linux has almost reached the level of Intel
linux.  It even looks like netscape is finally available for Alpha linux.

I ended up with a 533Mhz PC164LX system with 128MB DRAM,
Ultra/Wide/Fast PCI SCSI controller, 2GB SCSI disk, SCSI CDROM, ATX
mid-tower case, and Matrox Millenium video card.  The 164LX was, until
recently, Digital's top Alpha system.  I think there are a couple more
available now, plus Samsung has one.  I paid $1750 total for my system (no
monitor) with tax and shipping.  For perspective, I paid $1125 for my
motherboard and processor, which was a good price at the time.

Hope that helps somebody.  Email me if you want more information.

Dan Watson (watsond@ucdavis.edu)




