> Nice idea...this raises the question - what does one need to build an alpha
> system?  Obviously the motherboard and processor are special parts; what
> about everything else?	Do I need special RAM, or can I get the same
SDRAM
> that goes into a PII?  Do I need a special etherboard or video card, or do
> they just need to be PCI?  Do I need a special SCSI controller or hard drive?
>  What about case and power supply?

You can buy Alpha motherboards and CPUs from OEMs such as our company, Hard
Data Ltd. http://www.harddata.com  The current Alpha models based on UX and
LX boards, use standard PC parts, the term "standard" though, needs to be
addressed. ;-)

Systems built based on these boards, use ECC SDRAM, true parity, non-buffered,
10ns or faster, 100MHz DIMMs. They are a bit picky though and it can be tricky
and expensive in the long run for an end user to source their own RAM.

The other area of concern, is the power supply and enclosure. Although the LX
and UX are ATX form factor, this really only describes the "footprint" of the
board. These boards use a non-standard I/O plate for the enclosure. The power
supply is the most important aspect, the power supply sold in a typical
enclosure purchased from a PC shop simply will not cut the mustard, it does
not supply the correct power requirements and our years of experience with
these supplies is that they are prone to early failure - they are cheap and
we tear them out, throw them away. Even low cost PCs we build - this is the
first thing we do, remove the cheap power supply from the enclosure and
replace it with a quality supply complete with ball bearing fan. We also
install a secondary cooling system with a ball bearing fan.

It is for these reasons, that we recommend that as a minimum, you buy a bare
bones configuration from us, consisting of board/cpu, enclosure, power supply
and memory.

In terms of the other components, such as ethernet, scsi, video etc., a Linux
Alpha is pretty flexible, if you decide to purchase just the bare bones
configuration from us, I know that we certainly would be happy to help you in
determining whether or not your added cards are compatible.

Of course, we would also be happy to "Line Item" quote any configuration,
showing the costs of each component. We are price competitive on these
individual components and do not "mark them up" any higher than what you would
pay buying them piece meal.

Contrary to what some individuals may believe, it is not more expensive to
buy a pre-configured box at least not from Hard Data Ltd. anyway... however,
if you wish to build your own, (or more importantly, use existing spare
components) that option is there as well and we help our clients do just
this, every day.

> I'd be nice to think that I could buy *just* a motherboard and processor and
> get everything else from my local PC vendor, but if that were the case,
> wouldn't everyone have an Alpha? :)

exactly! ;-)


