Robert Harley <harley@corton.inria.fr> writes:

>uvatha@my-dejanews.com writes:
>> Everyone says "just go check out the microway specials"...

>Well I'd beg to differ!  I've been tracking prices advertised on the
>Web for about a year and *without a doubt* the best from that point of
>view are DCG Inc.  When I ask for quotes, HardData are the only ones
>to match them.

    I can confirm that with one exception: Both DCG and HardData are
    very responsive to email (the exception is that for some strange
    reason the Hard Data folks said they prefer to send quotes by fax
    but even after several reminders, the quote never arrived, so I
    ended up buying from LodgePole when they announced their $200 off
    Christmas special).

    There was also a curious technical controversy which had been
    bothering me and again it was the Hard Data guys who gave me what
    seemed to be a reasonable explanation: Several vendors were
    recommending NOT using a SCSI CDROM because they claimed it would
    result in slowing ALL SCSI devices down to the slowest device speed
    even when the CDROM was not in use. Others claimed this was bogus.

    Not being very knowledgeable about SCSI this really bothered me
    since I preferred a SCSI CDROM but I wasn't willing to have all my
    disk transfers slow down because of it.

    The explanation from Hard Data was as follows (fleshed out from
    other facts I picked up from reading the SCSI FAQ): There are two
    kinds of SCSI: LVD (low-voltage differential) and SE (single-ended).
    LVD uses two lines and the voltage differential between them is the
    signal which enables it to use lower voltages, greater cable
    distances and better performance. If LVD and SE devices are mixed,
    the entire bus reverts to SE mode thus negating all the benefits of
    LVD. Fortunately, LVD is only used on very high end machines and is
    not very common.

>Paradoxically, the most helpful people who reply to my queries, get
>back to me with details, who are up-front about what they have in
>stock and so on are... the same ones who have the best prices: it's a
>tie between Stephen at DCG Inc and Maurice at HardData!

    With almost all vendors, I found it very difficult to get them to
    provide details of parts on the quote: e.g.:
    -- make and model of CDROM,
    -- whether the case has a fan in addition to the CPU-fan and the
       power supply fan;
    -- make and model of hard drive;
    -- whether the hard drive carries a 5yr manufacturer's warranty
    -- whether the serial ports are 9-pin or 25-pin male or female
    -- whether the internal SCSI cable had any free connectors for
       adding devices later; if so how many;
    -- whether the Iomega Jaz drive includes a cartridge or not;

    Many are able to provide these sorts of details if you phone and
    are persistent but they seem very cavalier about email.

    One vendor (Microway) replied that they were just too busy to answer
    my list of email questions. I assumed they were not interested in
    single-unit sales which seems to be the case with many vendors.

    Ram



