Konrad Hinsen wrote:

> We are considering to buy some Linux/Alpha systems mainly for number
> crunching. What I am a bit concerned about is compiler quality. Do gcc
> and/or egcs give good performance for numerical code in C and C++? And
> are there any decent Fortran compilers (g77 not having the best
> reputation)?

In my opinion you can do it as well with a Pentium-II (or a dual Pentium-II).

We work with nuclear reactor calculations, and for this kind of number crunching
a PII-400MHz runs half as fast as the fastest Alpha, with the difference that the
PII system costs 30.000 FRF and the Alpha costs 1.6 Million francs. We measure
this by counting how many standard cases per hour we can run.

Depending on how many users will be calculating at the same time I would
suggest you to buy a cluster of PII or dual PII and run them with beowulf.

About compilers, egcs is very good and on the F77/F90 fron ABSoft Fortran
does a good work.

