No doubt this is a pci-64+ aka Cabrio system.

I'm going to throw in a plug for DCG here.  For 300+ship I bought the
275MHz Alpha in a "Dec" box (their OEM RIP system) that even has an
American made power supply.  I had a very hard time getting RAM for this
*beast*.  The first attempt was from my usual supplier, TheChipMerchant,
which supplies 12 chip SIMMs.  These work great in the UDB, but not in the
Cabrio.  I sent the board+RAM back to DCG and they tested the RAM to
verify it
was a no-go with the board.  (You will get six beeps at power on,
repeating.)  DCG is good as they provided this service without charge (nor
did they remind me that I am an alpha-newbe idiot.)

The trouble was finbding a place that would sell RAM and take it back if
it was not-compat, without charging a 15% re-stock fee.  CPU Micro Mart
was willing to do this.  They supplied 60ns gold plated 9 chip SIMMs that
just work great.  (They are Toshiba chips on Toshiba
fiberglass.)  $50.00/16MB, requires four in the Cabrio.  www.compusa.com
has better prices for kingston ($35.00/stick) but who knows if they will
work.

Since we're talking about building Alphas' from OTS parts, everyone has
told me that ide hard drives won't work with Linux/Alpha.  My Alpha is
running
RedHat 5.2 with a Wester Digital Caviar 6400 IDE and an IDE CD-ROM.  I
know you are thinking slow processor hogging IDE.  The Cabrio takes 1/2 as
long to search the file system on IDE as does a UDB using a 7200RPM
AtlasII on a SCSI-2 bus.  Hence the drive doesn't seem to be that big an
issue.

Stephen.



