Greetings to all,

I'm in an interesting situation... I'm a computer/net-enthusiast, (a
couple notches down from a "power-user" level). I have become intrigued
in Linux - my electrical engineer of a father has made a hobby of it but
I never got the bug until now.  The company I work for has a Alpha dust
collector.  They have no use for it, as NT is too bloated to run on it.
So, I thought it would make an interesting test case for the
power/efficiency of Linux/Alpha.  They want proof-positive that Linux
can make this machine live a little longer.  I'm actually working in
Poland (long story), and they have never heard of Linux before, as well
as many other technologies. They think MS is the only way to go. So,
they want to scrap the Alpha tower... I have an opportunity to prove
them wrong against their current webserver, a P2 266 mHz, running NT 4.
Would love to come back to this and post the uptime and performance
statistics of these two machines.

Frankly, I can use all the help I can get. Advice, Tips, URLs, etc, etc.
I will be checking back often and posting additional questions as I
proceed... hopefully others looking can learn as we go along?

--------------------------------
First, system description, this is all I have now... but will crack open
the case and supply any info if you tell me what to look for.

* Model Number: PB-22H-CX
* axp 150 mHz chip  * 32 megs RAM
* SCSI (EISA) card - looks like external port is SCSI-2, possibly fast?
* 2 SCSCI drives - 500 megs each - total 1 Gig.
      (Seagate ST3610N is one of them - other is hard to see,
        but assuming it is identical)
* cassette-based SCSI CD-ROM drive
* 10baseT NIC (EISA)
* 1.44 floppy - but the eject button says 2.88(?)
* low-level video card (EISA) - Compaq QVGA MVW is printed on the card.
       Some of the chips have NEC on them. But the biggest chip - the
       RAMDAC has "Bt" printed on it.
* simple, small monitor. 50-60 Hz, approx 12" viewable display area
* PS/2 keyboard & mouse

Personally, I have a external SCSI Jaz drive... and a couple spare 1 gig
disks I can use in the setup, install... and I know that it has worked
with the NT4 on the alpha, before it got screwed up.

--------------------------------
Second, special considerations...

They have valuable corporate data they would like to relocate from this
machine, so I think two installs are warrented. The first to be
none-destructive to the current data existing on the system with the
intial setup allowing a network transfer to another machine. Once
completed, a clean wipe of the two HDs, and a new reinstall to total
Linux.

This can not cost the company anything - their finances are in horrible
condition. Everything must be free. Hence, I must ftp the setup or
something... no purchase of distributions. I need recommendations on
this...  sites, online manuals, materials for newbies...   If a alpha
distribution on CD is cheap enough, they might swing for it. Then, I
would like recommendations on which distribution to purchase in Europe.

Currently, the system doesn't work. The tech guy screwed around with NT,
and now the machine doesn't load after initial boot. So, I probably need
to create some Linux boot floppies... ?

If the company decides not to do Linux, what would be a fair offer to
make to them for this system in US$???  Since I can not afford a new
system on a my Polish salary, an antique alpha system might make a nice
hobby Linux system to finess in spare time.

-------------------------------
Third, you should probably be aware of my skill level, for when you
detail directions.

I've tinkered with computers my whole life. My father being an engineer
built his own computers and I helped out. He's not in Poland with me
though...

I've been a light user of Unix, mostly for website administration and
publishing. I've used NT4 as my desktop  for years (and had so many
frustrating problems I'm surprised I have any hair left on my head).

I've programmed in perl, nothing complex - web robots, search engine.
I've manipulated server logs. Light stuff. Confidence is low on getting
heavy into Linux setup... but screw it, you only learn by jumping in and
doing it, right?

Thanks in advance for all help... I hope you are interested in the
results...

RNP




