In article <78ldks$h6t$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>,
  rclark@dotwireless.com wrote:
> Hi, I am trying to get IDE working on my UDB.
>
> The UDB came with a 44 pin cable connected to the connector block next to the
> SCSI connection on the PCI riser block.  Is this really the IDE connector,
> because there is also a 40 pin connector on the logic board.
>
> Does anyone have the pinout of the 44 pin connector?  There looks to be a
> power connector (at least that is what I think it is) next to the 44 pin
> connector on the riser.  Does the 44 pin connector carry power?
>

The 44 pin _IS_ the IDE connector.  The 4 right-most pins carry the power,
etc. (when facing the back of the drive.)

This is standard for 2-1/2" IDE.  Most laptops have these, but most have the
HD attached with a proprietary "quick-disconnect" cartridge or cable adaptor,
which renders the cable from this useless.  The IBM ThinkPad series comes to
mind.

These cables are most likely obtained from manufacturers and distributors of
hard-disk duplicators, as an accessory item.  I got mine from CSC
(www.corpsys.com.)

As far as a pinout, it's 44-pins straight through.  There's power on this, so
don't connect it backwards!

Jeremiah Cornelius

