Munagala V. S. Ramanath (ram@netcom.com) wrote:
:     The explanation from Hard Data was as follows (fleshed out from
:     other facts I picked up from reading the SCSI FAQ): There are two
:     kinds of SCSI: LVD (low-voltage differential) and SE (single-ended).
:     LVD uses two lines and the voltage differential between them is the
:     signal which enables it to use lower voltages, greater cable
:     distances and better performance. If LVD and SE devices are mixed,
:     the entire bus reverts to SE mode thus negating all the benefits of
:     LVD. 

Technically this is correct, but you should keep things is perspective.
Add LVD-capable drives I'd seen will run at Ultra speeds in single-ended
mode. Therefore, for 16-bit (wide) SCSI you'll be down to 40Mbytes/s
instead of 80Mbytes/s. A good fast drive will have peak media transfer
speed of some 10Mbytes/s, so that your SE chain will still be able to
accomodate four drives running full speed. 

So yes, you do have to worry about this - but only if you have more than
four pretty fast drives on your chain. 

/Serge.P



