In article <781meq$12mi@rzurs10.unizh.ch>,
	noe@physik.unizh.ch (Norbert Paschedag) writes:
> Hi, 
> 
> I have a question for the more experienced Fortran users : 
> 
> We're trying to run DU binaries (Dec F90) on our Linux machines. Under both
> Digital UNIX and Linux we experience floating point exceptions which 
> appear due to FP underflows etc.

In my C-source code I use under Linux and Digital Unix I use the
following:



#ifdef __osf__

void init_exceptions(void)
{

#else /* Linux */

/* Linux on Digital Alpha needs an initialisation of the ieee floating point
	control for IEEE compliant arithmetic (option -mieee of GCC). Under
	Digital Unix this is done automatically.
*/

#include <asm/fpu.h>

extern unsigned long ieee_get_fp_control();
extern void ieee_set_fp_control(unsigned long fp_control);

void init_exceptions(void)
{
/* initialize floating point control */

ieee_set_fp_control(ieee_get_fp_control()
                    & ~IEEE_TRAP_ENABLE_INV
                    & ~IEEE_TRAP_ENABLE_DZE
/*                  & ~IEEE_TRAP_ENABLE_UNF   we dont want underflow */
                    & ~IEEE_TRAP_ENABLE_OVF);
#endif
}

Try a similar initialisation using the FORTRAN equivalent.

Andi

