I have been scratching my head about this one for days.  Anyone seen it (I am
convinced that it is not an Alpha/Linux problem, but others around here are).

Here is the skinny:

Alpha LX running RedHat 5.2.  Working beautifully as a web farm server.  So far,
we have 74 aliases on the DEC Tulip NIC, all on a single class C.  Weird thing
happens when we tried to add another interface (eth0:75):
address will not route.  No access from anywhere, period (except the
localhost).  So I changed the IP, and viola, it works.  So you say, OK, the
first IP is in use elsewhere, or someone else is claiming it on the network. 
Not the case;  I have gone over our network with a fine toothed comb, and this
IP is not in use anywhere.  I even assigned it to another machine
(BSD-3.1/Intel), and it works there.  But it gets even more weird:  none of the
other addresses in this class C above the first (.172) will work on the Linux
machine, but they will route when assigned to other machines.  I know this
sounds like a network problem unrelated to Linux, but I swear to you it is not.
So the question is: kernel bug?  have I found some weird resource limit?

Any advice?

-Kevin Raison



