Hey there


Anselm Gademann wrote:

> I could start up X Windows (XFree86 Version 3.3.2.3-25) without ny major
> problems. I started it up with FVWM2. The funny thing was, that it is
> only starting the Desktop after I have moved the mouse. Is that normal?

Nope.

> I don't need to do it with my Pentium running Linux. But I could see the
> Window and I could also open 'glint'. I could see all the installed
> packages. I then mounted the CD, no problems there either, and tried to
> install a simple package. But I couldn't do it with glint. I could get a
> list with all the packages available on the CD (RedHat5.2 CD) but as
> soon as I tried to install a package, the window showing the progree
> appeared, but somehow it didn't want to start installing the thing. It
> didn't do anything at all and the only way to get it to close was to
> press the x in the top right hand corner. I then had to restart glint
> (version 2.6.2 by the way) and the program appeared as installed. I was
> happy with that, but then I tried to uninstall it with glint and he gave
> out the next error and I couldn't do that at all.

I had those problems as well. My advice: Don't use glint, it seems to be
broken. Just use the rpm command in a terminal.

> I then tried to install enlightment and KDE on the system as the Desktop
> instead of FVWM2 because I am using it on Intel Linux.

Ehm, enlightenment AND kde? I would pick only one of those (personally,
I prefer e), and only install the add-ons from the other one.

> I installed enlightment on it and it started up ok. It showed me an error message
> because of the audio which didn't bother me to much, because I had it on
> my other computer as well. But the when it was supposed to start up
> totally it somehow didn't want to do that. I got some strange not
> identifiable symbols at the left side and I didn't get any on the right
> side. The only way to cancle it was by using Ctrl+Alt+Backspace.

Here again, I had those problems. The first thing to do is to check for
libgr package. If it's there, get rid of it. This one is outdated and
broken. The seconf thing is to check for a wrong libpng version. 1.0.1
is the one you need. 1.0.2 is broken.

If you still have problems (as I had), I can't yet help you. I am about
to get e compiled and running (from CVS), but I am stuck with esound. By
the way, if anyone can help me there, I sent a mail about it last
wednesday.

> I then tried to install KDE. I downloaded the rpm packages and installed them
> with the rpm command (not with glint) they didn't gave me any error so I
> thought they should be all right. I edited my .Xclients file and it
> seemed that it was ok. When I then tried to start X Windows with startx,
> it started up ok and I heared that the screen switched to another
> resolution, but after turning black it switched back and told me that he
> can't find a couple of files. Do I have to edit another file to tell him
> where all these commands are? It was automtically done when I used the
> intel packages on my other computer?

I didn't have any trouble with kde. Check out in what order the packages
need to be installed. This should work fine.

> When I tried to remove KDE afterwards, rpm told me that it can't
> uninstall the package because it is not installed?! The directories were
> all there and when I started glint it also told me that they are
> installed. I then tried to install the packages again and I also tried
> to upgrade them and both times rpm told me that it can't because these
> packages are already installed. So I thought I just try to uninstall
> them again (using rpm -e kde...) But there it told me that they are not
> installed. So I had to messages on the screen which don't fit together
> at all.

Uhm. I use rpm -Uvh to install/upgrade. rpm -e to uninstall. Sometimes,
--nodeps is necessary, sometimes, --prefix=/usr/local/lib is good. And
there's a switch to force rpm to install outdated packages, which I use
sometimes (I forgot that one's name, check the manpage).

> Questions:

> 2. Do I have to add a path in a setup file which is normally done
> automatically? What and in which file?

Not whith kde. With enlightenment however, you have to set some things.
Read onenight.html from e's homepage to see what to do. This might not
be necessary if you use the packages instead of the sources.

> 3. Is there any way to uninstall the packages again? Is there any other
> package manager which will work or do I have to install them by 'hand'?

Reinstall with that switch whose name I forgot, add --nodeps, then
uninstalling should work.

> 4. Could it be that there is something wrong with my XFree installation?

Unlikely. For me, installing the latest rpms worked quite well, but I
needed to get freetype before that.

> Thanks in advance
> 
> Anselm

No problem, I hope this helps you

greets, Tobe



