Just looking for any clues.... 

The short version: My PC164/21164-433 system is playing (almost) dead. 
Applying power just brings up a black screen, unless the system is stripped
to the bare minimum (motherboard, VGA and keyboard), the power supply is a
300W one, and one has not tried it in a while. Even then, the system tends to
crash and burn with a "machine check" while mucking around in ARC

If you are in any position to make educated guesses for such a
situation, please continue to read the long version, which has all the
detail --- I _need_ some input. Pretty please? If you are not, thanks
for reading this far, and may the same never happen to you!

     Bernie

-----------------------------------
Long version:

Yesterday,I tried putting an old SCSI CD-ROM into my Alpha (PC164 motherboard,
433MHz 21164A, 128M memory, 2940UW, a few disks), and the whole thing turned
out to be rather unreliable and flaky --- even when I used a SCSI controller
just for the CD-ROM, and even when I used another CD-ROM. While looking for
a reason for this, I noticed that the fan in the power supply was having
a hard time turning at all, and was almost stopping and then speeding up
in something like a 2 second cycle. "Well", I thought, "that might put a bit
of strain on the 12V levels, and those old SCSI CD-ROMs probably need a 
steady 12V".

So I took the power supply apart, put a bit of lubricant on the fan spindle,
and lo and behold, it turns strong and fast. However, it also fails to make
my Alpha do _anything_ most of the time --- all I get is a black screen.

Now, this wouldn't be weird, if it wasn't for a number of observations:

1 _Sometimes_ I can get a stripped down system (just the motherboard, VGA card
  and keyboard) into ARC, and can actually do everything there. When that
  happens, sometimes it crashes after a while and brings up a machine check.
  When it happens, there are also two times when something (I _think_ it's
  the power supply, bot sure, though) emits a very high pitched whine. It
  ends very quickly, though, and is completely absent once the ARC turns up.
  Chances are increasing the longer one has left motherboard and power 
  supply to themselves before, i.e. the longer ago the last attempt has
  been.

2 The same power supply does a wonderful job at supplying a Celeron system.
  Reliably, every time. 

3 Out of two that I tried, no other (250W) power supply will even "boot" 
  that stripped down system. Unfortunately, no other 300W one is 
  available at the moment :-(

4 I have tried different keyboards and gfx cards, too. No difference

As I see it, (2), (3) and (4) point to the motherboard. However, (1)
clearly shows that nothing is fundamentally wrong. And the lack of any
chance of "booting" when either another PCI card is inserted _or_ a disk
is powered up seems to indicate a power problem. But then, those 250W
power supplies should easily be able to supply the MB, VGA and keyboard ---
while in reality, the 300W one sounds like it is very near its limits.
The whole thing, including all cards and 3 SCSI-UW disks used to run
off a 250W power supply for about 2 years.....

Whatever happens, the CPU fan keeps going, and the LED on the 2940UW
(when it is plugged in) keeps shining. So it doesn't seem to be a case
of the power supply simply switching off the power. I tried halving the
memory, and have cleaned 2.5 years of dust out of the machine, but all to
no avail. Frankly, I am at my wits' end, and am just hoping for someone to
point me in a new direction.

One more bit of information --- it is _possible_ (although anything but
certain) that I accidentally put the 3.5" floppy power connector on wrongly
when I put the power supply back in the machine (unfortunately, that
connector is hidden behind many ribbon cables, so I usually do this by touch.
And these things _are_ supposed to be coded!).

Any ideas? They would be most appreciated!

Ta,

    Bernie

