I had a bitch of a time trying to get mine to boot, and the installation
process hung several times at various stages. The fifth time it went through,
oddly enough, since I didn't change anything between tries. Buggy.
Also note that you want to have enough memory (I tried to install with 16
the first time and got the strangest errors), and use BSD disklabels instead
of the regular PC-style ones. Of course this caused my installation to crash
when it complained it couldn't read the disklabels after partitioning ->
reboot. Also, be quick to install aboot before the installation process has 
come to an end as it will happily restart without waiting for you. A lot
of useful info (and some erroneous one) can be found at the alpha addendum to
the red hat manual. This is again a case where you /really/ want to read the
instructions completely before starting. Twice.



