Sanjay Lal (sanjayl@home.com) wrote:
: the time to do a proper port.  I was wondering if gcc or egcs supported a 32
: bit mode similar to Digital's C/C++ compilers?

The -taso mode supported by Digital C is not really a 32-bit mode. The
only thing it does is to force all your code and data segments (including
anything coming from shared libs) to be located in the lower 31 bits of
the address space, which hides pointer truncation errors upon conversion
to an integer. Apart from this, it is identical to the regular 64-bit
compilation. At least for statically linked executables, you should be
able to achieve a similar effect using appropriately small settings for 
-T{bss|data|text} ld options.

/Serge.P



