In article <3H1n2.562$B21.116@news.rdc1.nj.home.com>, "Sanjay Lal" <sanjayl@home.com> writes:
|> I have several large pieces of code that are not 64 bit clean.
|> Unfortunately, I do not have
|> the time to do a proper port.  I was wondering if gcc or egcs supported a 32
|> bit mode
|> similar to Digital's C/C++ compilers?

Depending on the portability problems, some adaptions can be made very quickly:
If there are any long-declarations in the source, you should change them to int,
that fixes most inter-architecture-communication problems. If there are
data structures for callbacks/function pointers and they are declared as
an int, you can change them to long (64bit). I've managed to port some bigger
programs (eg. gimp<0.99.12) with these simple rules and some manual work...

