>>>>> "MD" == Mirko Doelle <m.doelle@dg2fer.link-goe.de> writes:

    MD> I've managed to pipe the console to the first serial port from
    MD> where I can interrupt the autoboot (running "minicom" on a
    MD> second computer connected with a Null-Modem-Cable). Problem:
    MD> The cursor keys don't work!

I'm assuming you are describing the ARC (Microsoft) console here, as
neither MILO nor SRM require the arrow keys to navigate.  As I recall,
ARC assumes a VT200 emulator when going through the serial port.  Try
a few terminals (or emulators) in the DEC VTxxx series and see what
happens.

    MD> I'm in need of either a tip how to get the keyboard work
    MD> without a video card or how to tell MILO to boot properly
    MD> without monitor.

Hmm...this was all very long ago, when I still had my UDB, but I
recall getting Linux to boot from ARC & MILO when the system was
headless.  As long as you set OSLOADOPTIONS correctly (in ARC, in the
boot menu configuration), MILO will automatically boot, keyboard or
no.  I also recall forcing ARC to use the serial device by setting the
CONSOLEIN and CONSOLEOUT environment variables to "multi()serial(0)"
(or something like that).

MILO shouldn't be too bothered by not finding a keyboard.  I don't
recall having any problems like that.  Also, you'll need to make
certain your kernel has serial console support (a config option in
recent development kernels).

Hope this helps.

