Depends on your bios I think.
Some systems use SRM, some AlphaBIOS.
I do not know SRM. I think it's kinda for Digital Unix setups.

If you have AlphaBIOS, you can point the OS selector to
MILO, and point MILO to the kernel (anywhere).
 
> And: How would i create such a thing? (i tried make zdisk
> like on x86, but this doesn't exist in the alpha version)

I take it you use MILO.
Well, MILO is pretty smart, smarter than LILO I would say.
It can boot from just about anything, any filesys, any
device, as long as MILO was compiled with the correct
drivers.

Just do a plain make, no opts, which will get you a vmlinux
You can compress this with gzip, and put it somewhere,
on a floppy disk, or on your root partition.

Then you simply point ALphaBIOS to MILO, and point MILO
to the kernel image. See milo docs for more precise
info.

