>>>>> "DSD" == David S Dandy <dandy@engr.colostate.edu> writes:

    DSD> The problem is that the date stamp on MANY files is exactly
    DSD> 20 years in the future --- Jan 8, 2019 or about such time.

    DSD> I swear I did nothing wrong...... honest.

This is a FAQ.  Search DejaNews; I've mentioned this before.

2.0 kernels don't automatically detect the starting epoch.  The year
is stored in memory as an offset from this epoch.  ARC counts years
starting at 1980, so the actual year is stored as "19".  On boot, 2.0
kernels assume the starting epoch is 1900 (i.e. you set the date from
the SRM console), and if the offset is less than 69, will
automatically add 100 to the date.

So the kernel reads "19", thinks "1919", and adds 100 to get "2019".

Editing /etc/sysconfig/clock after you install isn't going to fix
anything, unless you re-install all the packages (which you can do
with RPM's "--replacepkgs" option).  You'll need to fool the kernel
into getting the correct date when you go to install the system.  Set
the date to the year 2079 (which is the ARC epoch plus 99 years); the
2.0 kernel will decide it is 1999, which is correct.

