From: rsimpson@ewrcsdra.Cut_This_Out.demon.co.uk
Subject: LX164 IDE beta testers needed
Date: 1999/01/08
Message-ID: <70q477.jd5.ln@snow_white>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 430201532
Organization: Defence Evaluation & Research Agency
User-Agent: tin/pre-1.4-980618 (UNIX) (Linux/2.0.36 (i686))
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha
Summary: Beta testers needed for new CMD646 driver

As many of you will be aware, the 2.1 kernels provide an option to enable
DMA support on the CMD646 IDE controller used on LX164 motherboards. 
Activating this option can significantly increase disc speed (x2.5 on my
system).

Unfortunately, the driver was written for SPARCs and this produces two
problems, one minor and the other more significant.

The minor problem is that the SPARCs use the very slightly different 646U
(which is supposed to support UltraDMA).  A few very minor code changes are
needed to accurately reflect the 646.

The big problem is that UltraSPARCs come with a very restricted set of IDE
devices.  The driver therefore sets the timing of both ports to PIO mode 4
and older devices (like my CDROM drive) cannot keep up.

I have produced a new version of the 646 driver which uses code based on
routines in the CMD640 driver to allow automatic setting of the correct
timing parameters based on values probed from the IDE devices (with a manual
override).  This new version is currently with the maintainer, but he can
only check that it still works OK on his SPARC.  I therefore need a few beta
testers who can check it out on their Alphas.

To undertake this task you need:
1) A machine with a CMD646 IDE controller (I don't know if it is used on any
Alphas other than the LX164).
2) To have some reasonably old IDE devices which request PIO modes < 4.
3) To be running a late 2.1.x kernel or a 2.2.0preX kernel.
4) To not mind if a bug hoses all of the data on your hard drive.
5) To be prepared to send PIO mode requests to the driver and report back
the diagnostic output.

If you are interested please email me at my home address below.

Thanks,

-- 
