broken.
: >>Period.  On any platform despite an occasional success you may have
when
: >>using those tools.
: >
: >Then I must have had many occasional success-es on my Intel box with
: >make menuconfig. Never noticed any problem there.

: >What's the problem on Intel ??

: Just another quick thought !!?? Can it really be that it's not 64-bit
clean ??

No.  It has nothing to do with that.  Hang around linux-kernel list
for a while and you will find out pretty soon what is wrong with it. :-)

The main trouble seems to be that both menuconfig and xconfig are
extremely fragile.  They may work ok in a given set of circumstances
and go haywire if something was a bit different.  From time to time on
linux-kernel people are posting "fixes" for configuration problems.
Normally they are "local" in that sense that they "fix" whatever the
patch author had in mind when s/he created patches.  But usually they
break horribly other things not touched by the poster as they were not
needed on this particular occasion.  Then Michael Elizabeth Chastain 
<mec@shout.net>, who wrangles with these issues, explains once again an
error in their ways; sometimes in a rather testy manner.  :-)

: Who would write such a program for a simply menu system ??? 

There are complicated interdependencies hidden in the whole thing.
It will get eventually rewritten as the work is going on.  Still
you do not want to make this thing to work only in **the current**
situation.  Linux kernel evolves all the time.

If you plan to put some effort in this first check with mec.

  Michal




