Toon Moene wrote:
> 
> Joachim Wesner wrote:
> 
> > Bram wrote:
> 
> > > I've been reading DEC's Assembly Language Programmer's Guide.
> > > But I have a couple of questions.
> 
> > > How can I have egcs generate more verbose assembly output?
> > > The samples in the guide show the C-source as comments in
> > > the assembly file.
> > > The output from egcc -S does not give these.
> 
> > To my knowledge, there is no way to have the assembly output mixed with
> > the C-source on gcc/egcs.
> 
> Well, I never got around writing this, but the egcc -g -S [other
> options] ... command should write the debugging information in the
> assembler file, and hence - because the debugging info contains the line
> number - it's possible (writing a small C program that reads both the .s
> and the .c file) to create a combined file with the assembler code
> interspersed with the original C code.

Well, one could either write a large C program or a small perl script.
This one takes the output of "gcc -g -S" and adds the corresponding
source to the line information in the assembler output.
I wrote this after just looking at some -S output, so it might be
complete crap. It just worked with what I tried ( including code from an
include file in a different directory)

#!/usr/bin/perl
#combines output of gcc -S -g with C source
# by Alex Rhomberg <rhomberg@ife.ee.ethz.ch>
#usage:
# gcc -S -g --otheroptions blops.c
# combasm blops.s

while (<>) {
  chomp;
  # file name and directory information are in the .stabs line
  # in double quotes
  #example:
  #.stabs "/home/me/progs/tmp/",100,0,0,$Ltext0
  #.stabs "blops.c",100,0,0,$Ltext0
  #here, I search for .stabs followed by a name, and finally $Ltext
  if (m|^\.stabs \"(.*)\"[\s\d,]+\$Ltext\d+|) { 
    $name = $1;
    if ($name =~ m|/$|) {
      #if the name ends in / it is the working directory
      chdir $name or die "couldn't chdir to $name";
    } else {
      #otherwise it is the source file, which is read and chomped
      open CFILE, "$name" or die "couldn't open $name";
      @cfile = <CFILE>;
      chomp @cfile;
      close CFILE;
    }
  }
  #line information lines start with .stabn, to numbers and the line
number
  #if such a line is encountered, the source line is added
  #in optimized code, the same line could appear several times
  if (/^\s+\.stabn \d+,\d+,(\d+)/) {
    $_ .= "\t#" . $cfile[$1-1];
  }
  print "$_\n";
}



