"Steve Lionel" <Steve.Lionel@digital.com> writes:

> The issues are still under review.  However, I want to point out that it's
> not merely a case of saying "yes" and magically, a compiler appears next
> week.  An Alpha Linux port of our compiler would require significant new
> engineering work due to the different linking, debugging and OS feature
> environment of Linux.  Just for starters, Alpha Linux uses ELF for its
> object language, while DIGITAL UNIX uses COFF.  We don't currently generate
> ELF on any of our platforms.  Even if a decision was made today to go ahead,
> you wouldn't see anything before next Christmas at the very earliest.
> 
> It would be useful to know why you want to use Linux instead of DIGITAL UNIX
> on Alpha. Is price of the software the only issue?

One of the biggest issues is the libraries. If DEC/Compaq decide to
design a chip without trancendentals and to rely on fast libraries
then they have to include the libraries in the price of the
chip. There is no point in selling a crippled chip cheap and saying
"if you want to be able to run it at full speed you have to give us a
load more money".  Compaq have to decide whether they want to sell
Alphas or not. If they do, license the libraries free for all
Alphas. If not, bye bye Alpha.

John



