Steve Lionel (Steve.Lionel@digital.com) wrote:
: environment of Linux.  Just for starters, Alpha Linux uses ELF for its
: object language, while DIGITAL UNIX uses COFF.  We don't currently generate
: ELF on any of our platforms.  Even if a decision was made today to go ahead,

In all respect, COFF vs. ELF does not count as a honest argument. Linux/Alpha
can and does run COFF executables just fine - provided that you either link
them statically or copy the required shared libraries from a Digital Unix
system. Either approach may run afoul of the local copyright laws and the 
terms of the DU licence[*] (and even the hardware used Linux is running on), 
but from the purely technical point of view it certainly works.

Regards,

/Serge.P

[*] My understanding is that running statically linked executables is Ok
as long as you have a valid DU licence for the machine running Linux/Alpha
(which makes the whole excersize a bit pointless).



