Andy Isaacson (adisaacs@mtu.edu) wrote:
: Well, if you want the lower overhead of Linux, or if you need some
: other feature that DUNIX doesn't provide (perhaps a custom kernel
: modification) or if DUNIX doesn't support some piece of hardware then
: it could make sense.  I suppose you'd have to reboot into DUNIX to
: compile anything, though...

Overhead, feachures, kernel development, and hardware support do not
really come into this. That really does is *COST* - Digital Unix licences
are expensive, and if you are running a compute farm you basically have
to use systems licensed to run DU if you want decently optimizing Fortran 
compiler. For our codes, for example, the difference between g77/egcc and 
Digital Fortran is over 30% in the execution time of the resulting 
executable. Having a native Linux version of Digital Fortran, or even a 
legal way of running executables compiled under Digital Unix on Linux 
machines not licensed to run DU might have allowed us to use cheaper 
third-party hardware... I guess this is not the best argument for convincing
Compaq management to release Linux version of f90, or is it?

Cheese,

/Serge.P



